        <div class="cav">The locked drawer</div>
        <h2 id="history">How far back does Citi keep your statements, and how do you get older ones?</h2>
        <p>Citi keeps your last twelve months of card statements available online, and archives anything older for retrieval on request. Citi's own paperless page states that "both your old (last 12 months) and new statements are available online," and that you can "request PDFs of past statements" beyond that at no cost (<a href="https://www.cardbenefits.citi.com/en/Products/Paperless-Statements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Citi</a>). Those older requests are not instant: Citi notes that "requests may take 48 to 72 hours to fulfill," and, importantly, that a "requested older statement is only available for 7 days" once it is ready (<a href="https://www.citi.com/CRD/PDF/Request_previous_statements_PDF.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Citi</a>). That short window is the practical reason to download and keep your own PDFs, and to convert them to a spreadsheet you control while you have them. A converter that reads the PDF is what turns any of those saved months into rows you can total.</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Save it while it is there</div><p>When Citi fulfills a request for an older statement, you have seven days before it disappears again. Download the PDF right away, then convert it to Excel or CSV so the history lives in a file you keep, not on a countdown.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Know the page</div>
        <h2 id="structure">What is on a Citi statement, and what comes across?</h2>
        <p>A Citi credit card statement is organized into the same blocks every month, and the tool's job is to lift the transaction detail out of them cleanly. Citi's own guide to reading a statement lists the sections: your basic account details and billing dates, an account activity summary with previous balance, payments, purchases, fees and new balance, the payment information box with your minimum due and due date, the itemized transactions, the interest-charge and fee detail with year-to-date totals, and a rewards summary where it applies (<a href="https://www.citi.com/credit-cards/understanding-credit-cards/what-is-credit-card-statement-and-how-to-read-it" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Citi</a>). The converter pulls the itemized transactions into date, description, and amount, and reconciles their sum to the printed purchases total so you can trust the export. The summary and interest sections stay on the statement as the source of truth you check against.</p>
