        <div class="cav">The payoff</div>
        <h2 id="tax">What is Citi statement data worth at tax time?</h2>
        <p>For anyone with business expenses, a clean spreadsheet of card charges is exactly what the IRS asks for. IRS Publication 463 states that "you must have documentary evidence" for expenses, and that acceptable proof is <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p463" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"a canceled check, credit card receipt, or written statement showing the name of the vendor, the date, and the amount."</a> You generally need that evidence for every expense of 75 dollars or more, and for all lodging. One caution worth stating plainly: a statement proves that you paid, but "proof of payment of an amount, by itself, does not establish you are entitled to a tax deduction," so you still pair the line with a receipt or invoice showing the business purpose (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p583" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Publication 583</a>). The IRS also recommends keeping records generally for <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">three years</a>. A reconciled export gives you the vendor, date, and amount triple, one row per charge, ready to sort into Schedule C categories.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you keep</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Vendor, date, amount</td><td>The three fields IRS Pub 463 names as documentary evidence, one per row</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">A matching receipt</td><td>Proves the business purpose the statement alone does not (Pub 583)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Three years of records</td><td>The general retention period the IRS asks for, longer in specific cases</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Your own saved PDFs</td><td>Insurance against Citi's 12-month online window and 7-day request expiry</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">While you are reviewing, watch for errors too</div><p>A sorted export makes unauthorized or duplicate charges easy to spot. If you find one, you have a deadline: both the CFPB and the FTC say you must send a written billing-error notice within 60 days after the statement with the error was sent (<a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/credit-cards/how-to-fix-mistakes-in-your-credit-card-bill/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>). A spreadsheet history makes that far easier to catch in time.</p></div>

